{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1023 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "48925ae632d315b071059623e557ea8f1684aed21f4838d42c2edfeb8747430b" :edb/db-before-t 1022 :edb/db-after-t 1023 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534335 50 #inst "2026-10-06T16:18:05.995Z" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092012 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-5)$\n\n- $(4,-1)$\n\n- $(-5,-3)$" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092012 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-5,-1), (1,4), (3,-5)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ about the origin?\n\n1. $(1,-5)$\n2. $(4,-1)$\n3. $(-5,-3)$" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092012 1145 "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ$. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRX &= \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092012 1145 "The clockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by an angle of $90^\\circ$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $270^\\circ.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{-90^\\circ}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-1,5), (4,-1), (-5,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092013 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5)$, and $(4,-3)$. Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S'$, the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n1. $(-2,1)$\n2. $(-4,3)$\n3. $(-5,3)$" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092013 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(2,-1), (-3,5),$ and $(4,-3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(-2,1)$\n\n- $(-4,3)$\n\n- $(-5,3)$" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092013 1145 "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092013 1145 "The counterclockwise rotation $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by an angle of $\\pi$ radians is given by the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S$ as columns:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf{R}_{\\pi}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nRX &= \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,1), (3,-5), (-4,3)$.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092061 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/a43a1658a16151c0306702959c50e8d0288077983ad50a084b39703e206469c0.png)\n\nConsider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092061 1100 "Consider the vector $\\mathbf{v}$ shown above. Find the vector $A\\mathbf{v}$ given that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/38d3b5868bf5043351514f3dff7dd033.png)" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092061 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{ \\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/414cb72ff004fe4c5898a0e2c7b60b4b3651473e776388fad21efd8fc9ae6165.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v},$ we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/b24d8d460b7833a49f2bd7183f0f4b5d22d94a7d3b1d3bb9eb51beff9eeb7ef6.png)" 13194139534335 true] [17592186092061 1145 "Consider the standard basis $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ of the Euclidean plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of $\\{\\mathbf{i},\\mathbf{j} \\}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^{′} &= Ai = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = j \\\\\nj^{′} &= Aj = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} =-i\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/2d169b5fa006402034a1f405a9c1cb45.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $A\\mathbf{v}$, we need to rotate $\\mathbf{v}$ counterclockwise by an angle of\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nradians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/931/Source/Images/12a8c4243623a4dea1baaca392b2af92.png)" 13194139534335 false] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283648 13194139534335 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283683 13194139534335 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283690 13194139534335 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283739 13194139534335 true] [17592186092170 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283746 13194139534335 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283655 13194139534335 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283662 13194139534335 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283697 13194139534335 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283704 13194139534335 true] [17592186092171 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283711 13194139534335 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283669 13194139534335 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283676 13194139534335 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283718 13194139534335 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283725 13194139534335 true] [17592186092219 1020 #edb/ref 17592186283732 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1017 #edb/ref 17592186283649 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1089 "q-12133" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at  $(-2,-4),(1,2),(2,-1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians about the origin?\n\n- $(2,1)$\n- $(4,-2)$\n- $(-1,2)$" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1113 #uuid "573337c1-dc60-59f2-9f19-17fbe28d3ce0" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283648 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ radians is the same as counterclockwise rotation by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-3\\pi /2},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-3\\pi /2}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(4,-2),(-2,1),(1,2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283650 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283651 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283652 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283653 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283654 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1041 "selection" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283654 13194139534335 true] [17592186283649 1110 #uuid "0b469864-d021-5341-965b-aa7b7dc92450" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283650 1034 "II and III only" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283650 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534335 true] [17592186283650 1111 #uuid "5c64b183-f6be-550e-bf79-ab7c4c78b1f8" 1319413953