97 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534335 true] [17592186283697 1100 "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,1),(0,-3),(-1,3).$  Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the **clockwise** rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(1,-3)$\n- $(-4,-1)$\n- $(0,3)$" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283697 1113 #uuid "b53733ad-39f5-5883-8ad6-7ec3f54d5d59" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283697 1145 "The clockwise rotation ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is the same as counterclockwise rotation by the same angle. It's given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{-{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-1),(0,3),(1,-3).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II and III.\"" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283699 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283700 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283701 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283702 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283703 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1041 "selection" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283699 13194139534335 true] [17592186283698 1110 #uuid "eef1e80e-f42c-524c-a