] [17592186283718 1145 "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =A(i)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i \\\\ {j}^{′} & =A(j)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/4e0e5c9cee037bded1f5739af35c3a8acfaa4d4e6f469b3e50afe69b96990e39.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\pi $ radians (counterclockwise or clockwise) about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ (counterclockwise or clockwise) by an angle of $\\pi $ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/45f0ff53440247c533dd2b97ae03c8cd3e2e762b67832ce770c0bd5749e74f6e.png)" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283720 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283721 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283722 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283723 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1000 #edb/ref 17592186283724 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1041 "selection" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1104 #edb/ref 17592186283721 13194139534335 true] [17592186283719 1110 #uuid "5a85a070-43e5-5b3f-b15a-8b65d3878416" 13194139534335 true] [17592186283720 1034 "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13991115/assets/ca19bbcea3f01e99b0c606ad719686d84bca7b4dbfa1596f7b28e27fa494c44c.png" 1319413